## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/pageService.ts** (`getAllPublishedPages`): This is the server-side service that FETCHES content from Contentstack via getEntry calls. The ContentstackContext/useContent hook only READS already-fetched content — the endpoint selection (published CDN vs preview/draft) is determined here at fetch time. The method name itself ('getAllPublishedPages') reveals it only targets the published CDN. The getEntry calls at lines 49 and 59 (and similarly in getHeaderAndFooter at lines 104-105) are where the Contentstack SDK is invoked, and the SDK's configuration decides which endpoint (delivery vs preview) is hit. The fix must ensure that when a preview signal is present, getEntry routes to Contentstack's preview endpoint instead of the published CDN.
  - **Minimal fix:** The getEntry function (called throughout PageService) must accept and forward a preview flag (derived from a URL query parameter or cookie) to the Contentstack SDK so it resolves against the preview/draft host instead of the published CDN host. In getAllPublishedPages, getHeaderAndFooter, getSingletonEntry, and other methods, detect the preview signal and pass it into getEntry. The Contentstack SDK supports preview by setting the host to the preview endpoint (e.g., 'cdn.contentstack.com' → 'preview.contentstack.com') and including a preview token — the getEntry function or the underlying SDK initialization must conditionally set these. The minimal change: add a preview parameter to getEntry calls and propagate the preview host/token from the request context (available in getStaticProps via context.preview or cookies) through PageService into the SDK client configuration.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- With a preview signal present (preview URL query parameter or cookie) on a GO, UP, or MX site page, the rendered page displays the latest draft/unpublished version of the page content as saved in the CMS, rather than the last published version.
- When a content editor saves a draft change to a page in the CMS and then views that page on the GO, UP, or MX site with the preview signal present, the rendered page shows the newly saved draft change without the content having been published.
- With no preview signal present (no preview query parameter or cookie), a page on the GO, UP, or MX site renders the currently published version of its content correctly, with all published text, images, and layout intact.
- When a draft edit is made to a page's body content in the CMS and the page is viewed with the preview signal present, the draft edit is visible in the rendered page body to the person viewing it.
- When draft edits are made to shared page regions (header and footer) in the CMS and any page is viewed with the preview signal present, those draft edits are visible in the rendered header and footer of the page.
- Adding the preview signal to a page visit switches the rendered content to the draft version, and removing the preview signal and reloading the same page returns the rendered content to the published version.
- With the preview signal present, every region and component of the rendered page (body content, header, footer, and any other CMS-driven sections) consistently displays draft content, with no region still showing the older published version.

